# Line endings.
#
# This repo builds on Windows and deploys through GitHub Pages on Linux. Without this file, Git
# falls back to core.autocrlf=true, which means every text file is CRLF in the working tree and LF
# in the repository, and every generator in scripts/ rewrites its output file on every run.
#
# That combination produced a false alarm that cost a deploy: templates/404.template.html was
# reported modified by `git status` while its bytes were identical to HEAD once line endings were
# normalised, and deploy.sh's source-tree guard refuses to deploy on a dirty tree. Every other run
# that day passed, so it looked intermittent. See the guard's own comment in deploy.sh.
#
# eol=lf keeps the working tree LF, so a file written by Python on Windows matches the repository
# instead of being converted twice. text=auto lets Git decide what is text - it will not touch
# binaries, and anything that looks binary is left alone.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Never rewrite, never normalise. These are bytes we generate or consume, and a stray conversion
# would corrupt them silently rather than fail loudly.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary

# Fonts and data assets the site serves verbatim.
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run on the Linux runner. They must be LF or bash fails on a stray CR.
*.sh    text eol=lf
